# =============================================================================
# .gitignore  --  files Git should never track
#
# Anything matching a line below is invisible to Git. It stays on your own
# computer but is never committed, never pushed, and never visible to anyone
# else on GitHub.
#
# Lines starting with ! are EXCEPTIONS -- "ignore everything except this."
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATA  --  the most important rule in this file
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Research data does not belong on GitHub. Data lives on Google Drive while a
# study is active and on Forge once it is archived. See data/README.md.
#
# Two reasons this matters more than it might seem:
#   1. Once a file is committed, it is in the repository's history FOREVER.
#      Deleting it later does not remove it. If the repository is ever made
#      public, everything ever committed becomes public with it.
#   2. GitHub rejects files over 100 MB and complains about anything over 50 MB.
#
# The rules below ignore everything inside data/ while still keeping the folder
# structure and the README files, so the folders exist when someone clones.

data/**
!data/**/
!data/README.md
!data/**/README.md
!data/**/.gitkeep

# Data files anywhere else in the project, in case one wanders out of data/
*.csv
*.tsv
*.xlsx
*.xls
*.sav
*.dta
*.por
*.rds
*.RDS
*.RData
*.rda
*.feather
*.parquet
*.sas7bdat

# If you have a small, FULLY DE-IDENTIFIED file that genuinely should be
# tracked -- an analytic dataset under a few MB that makes your results
# reproducible -- you can override these rules for that one file:
#
#     git add -f data/processed/analysis_sample.csv
#
# Do this deliberately, never in bulk, and only after confirming the file
# contains nothing that could identify a participant. See
# docs/before-going-public.md.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECRETS  --  API keys, tokens, passwords, connection strings
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Same permanence problem as data: a committed key is a leaked key, even if you
# delete it in the next commit. Keep credentials in .Renviron or .env, which are
# ignored here, and never paste them into a script.

.Renviron
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
credentials.*
secrets.*
service-account*.json
token.json
.httr-oauth


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# R
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.Rhistory
.Rapp.history
.RData
.Ruserdata
.Rproj.user/
*.Rproj.user
.Rprofile.user

# renv: the library folder is huge and machine-specific. renv.lock IS tracked
# (it records which package versions you used) but the packages themselves
# are not.
renv/library/
renv/local/
renv/cellar/
renv/lock/
renv/python/
renv/staging/

# Knitr / Quarto / R Markdown build products
*_cache/
/cache/
*_files/
/*_files/
.quarto/
_site/
_book/
*.utf8.md
*.knit.md


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python  (harmless to keep even in an R project)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
env/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stata  (harmless to keep even in an R project)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.smcl
*.stpr


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LaTeX build products
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.aux
*.bbl
*.blg
*.fdb_latexmk
*.fls
*.out
*.synctex.gz
*.toc


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Operating system clutter
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# macOS
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes

# Windows
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/

# Office lock files -- created while a Word or Excel file is open
~$*
.~lock.*


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Miscellaneous
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.log
*.tmp
*.bak
*.swp


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OUTPUT  --  folders are kept, contents are not
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated figures, tables, and model objects stay on your machine. The
# folders themselves are kept so scripts have somewhere to write.
#
# The reason is not obvious and is worth stating: a fitted model object is not
# a summary of your data, it CONTAINS your data. An lm() object carries a
# $model component holding the complete model frame -- every row that went into
# the fit. Saving one to output/ and committing it would put participant-level
# data into the repository's permanent history, in a file that looks like a
# result rather than a dataset.
#
# Rather than trying to sort the safe outputs from the unsafe ones, nothing in
# output/ is tracked. Everything here is regenerable by running
# code/run_all.R, so nothing is lost.

output/**
!output/**/
!output/README.md
!output/**/README.md
!output/**/.gitkeep

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REPORTS  --  tracked, because they are written to be read
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# reports/ holds rendered summaries meant for collaborators and PIs who do not
# run R. Unlike output/, these ARE tracked -- the whole point is that someone
# can read them on github.com without installing anything.
#
# Quarto writes a report's figures into a folder like reports/summary_files/,
# which the *_files/ rule above would otherwise ignore, leaving broken images.
# These lines let a report's own figures through.

!reports/**/*_files/
!reports/**/*_files/**

# ...but not Quarto's HTML scaffolding. Quarto writes Bootstrap, tippy,
# clipboard.js and friends into libs/ even when the report renders to gfm --
# roughly 900K of assets a Markdown report never references. Figures sit
# beside libs/, not inside it, so re-ignoring this folder costs nothing.

reports/**/*_files/libs/

# The data rules still apply inside reports/: a stray .csv or .rds in this
# folder stays ignored. Only the report and its figures are tracked.


# If you need to share a specific figure or an aggregated table -- for a
# collaborator who does not run R, or for a journal's supplementary materials
# -- you can override this for that one file:
#
#     git add -f output/figures/fig1_outcome_by_condition.png
#
# Before you do, confirm the file shows aggregated results only. A figure
# plotting every individual case in a small sample can identify participants
# even though it contains no names.
